  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 330 AM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe long term period begins with the passage of a cold front through \nthe area, ushering in the coldest air so far this fall from Friday \nnight through the weekend. High pressure will continue to build \nbehind this front as a 1028 mb surface high shifts southward out of \nCanada into the Midwestern states by early next week. As a result, \nwe can expect very dry conditions to persist, with PW values below \n0.25 inches and no chance of rainfall through Sunday. As the surface \nridge builds closer to the area, expect surface winds from the \nnorthwest to diminish from prior days to around 5-10 mph on \nSaturday, with a few afternoon gusts up to 18 mph, before becoming \nlighter from the northeast on Sunday. \n\nAs mentioned, a few colder days can be expected with daytime high \ntemperatures ranging from 8-10 degrees below average through the \nweekend. This will result in highs in the low to upper 50s across \nmost of the area, while the mountains will struggle to get into the \nupper 40s, before warming some into the first part of next week. \nOvernight lows will drop to near or below freezing for the entire \nCWA, dipping into the mid to upper 20s over all of north Georgia on \nSunday morning. \n\nMoving into Monday, plenty of mid to high clouds will spill into the \nregion as guidance showcases a shortwave kicking out of the Southern \nPlains on Sunday and diving toward the Southeast in support of a \npossible coastal low, which may spin up a few showers over our \ncentral Georgia counties Monday night into Tuesday. Right now, \nshower chances remain limited and dependent on the development and \ntrack of the low. Additionally, with the surface high transiting \nover the Ohio Valley, surface winds will be out of the northeast \nover the area, establishing a wedge-type environment and reinforcing \nthe drier, stable conditions. Regardless, any rain that does manage \nto fall would be light, with guidance coming into better agreement \non keeping the more appreciable rainfall potential south of the \nFlorida Panhandle. Beyond this, benign and seasonable mid November \ncondition will continue into Wednesday.",
